<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <data>
        <record id="module_category_nrs_assets" model="ir.module.category">
            <field name="name">NRS Asset Register</field>
            <field name="sequence">35</field>
        </record>

        <!--
            WHY THESE TWO RECORDS EXIST (handoff 41).

            On Odoo 19 a group is placed on the user form through a
            `res.groups.privilege`, not through `category_id` — `res.groups`
            no longer has that field at all. The form widget
            (`web/static/src/webclient/res_user_group_ids_field/`) renders one
            SELECTION per privilege, listing that privilege's groups as
            mutually exclusive options under a "No" placeholder, grouped by
            the privilege's category. A group with NO privilege is rendered
            only under "Extra Rights", and only when developer mode is on —
            which is exactly how the owner found these three: invisible on
            every user form, assignable only from the Groups menu in debug.

            So the category above was empty for the whole life of the module,
            and the fix is two privileges, not one:

            * "Asset Register" holds Asset User, Asset Officer and Asset
              Manager. They are one choice — Manager implies Officer implies
              User — so a selection is the right control, and the widget
              orders them by implication.
            * "Tag Issuing" holds the Tag-Issuing Unit ALONE. If it sat in
              the same privilege as the other two, the form could not give
              one person Asset User AND Tag-Issuing Unit, because a privilege
              is a single selection — and that pairing is how a human tagger
              is provisioned (see the comment on the group below, and
              `docs/DECISIONS.md`, 2026-10-01, handoff 41).

            This is DISPLAY GROUPING ONLY. A privilege creates no implication
            and no access rule; the Tag-Issuing Unit stays implied by nothing
            and implying nothing. Do not "tidy" the two privileges into one.
        -->
        <record id="privilege_asset_register" model="res.groups.privilege">
            <field name="name">Asset Register</field>
            <field name="sequence">10</field>
            <field name="category_id" ref="module_category_nrs_assets"/>
            <field name="description">What this person may do in the asset register.</field>
        </record>
        <record id="privilege_tag_issuing" model="res.groups.privilege">
            <field name="name">Tag Issuing</field>
            <field name="sequence">20</field>
            <field name="category_id" ref="module_category_nrs_assets"/>
            <field name="description">Whether this person may confirm that a printed label is physically on the asset it names. Separate from the register role on purpose: pair it with Asset User for a human tagger.</field>
        </record>

        <record id="group_asset_user" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Asset User</field>
            <field name="privilege_id" ref="privilege_asset_register"/>
            <field name="sequence">10</field>
            <field name="comment">Can view assets, log verifications, raise requests.</field>
        </record>

        <!--
            The role between reading the register and running it (handoff
            47, ruling 3). Handoff 46's persona matrix measured the gap: a
            store officer could look at the register but not register an
            asset, request a move or record a verification — and the only
            role that could do those things could also approve other
            people's moves and change the branding, the depreciation rules
            and the SAP integration. This group is the storekeeper's actual
            job, composed of scopes that already existed (`assets:write` on
            top of Asset User's reads — see GROUP_SCOPES in
            myservice_api/controllers/utils.py). It deliberately does NOT
            carry `approvals:write` (the second person on every move is the
            point of handoff 35), `settings:write`, `integrations:write`,
            `locations:write` or `ingestion:write`.

            Both implications below are spelled out explicitly — Manager
            lists Officer AND User even though Officer already implies User —
            so a reader of this file (and tests/test_login_scopes.py, which
            resolves one level) sees the whole ladder without computing a
            closure.
        -->
        <record id="group_asset_officer" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Asset Officer</field>
            <field name="privilege_id" ref="privilege_asset_register"/>
            <field name="sequence">15</field>
            <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(4, ref('group_asset_user'))]"/>
            <field name="comment">Registers assets, requests moves and custody changes, records verifications. Does not approve other people's requests, configure the tenant or run imports.</field>
        </record>

        <record id="group_asset_manager" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Asset Manager</field>
            <field name="privilege_id" ref="privilege_asset_register"/>
            <field name="sequence">20</field>
            <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(4, ref('group_asset_user')), (4, ref('group_asset_officer'))]"/>
            <field name="comment">Full access — create, edit, transfer, dispose, approve, manage PM schedules and configuration.</field>
        </record>

        <!--
            The Tag-Issuing Unit (INT-04/05). This group is the ONLY grant of
            the `tagging:write` API scope, and it is deliberately implied by
            nothing: Asset Manager must not pick it up, because the team that
            creates asset records must not also be the team that confirms a
            label went onto the metal. An affix confirmation submitted by the
            credential that created the asset proves only that one system
            said so twice.

            Kept out of `implied_ids` in BOTH directions on purpose — do not
            "tidy" this by hanging it under Asset Manager.

            A HUMAN in this group holds it alongside Asset User. The group
            grants the `tagging:write` scope and no `ir.model.access` row,
            which is complete for the API credential it was built for (every
            route runs sudo(), the scope is the gate) and incomplete for a
            person, who also needs to read the register they are tagging.
            Handoff 41 drove the station as a user holding ONLY this group
            and chose to document the pairing rather than add a read rule
            that would duplicate what Asset User already says — the reasons
            are in `docs/DECISIONS.md` (2026-10-01, handoff 41).
        -->
        <record id="group_tag_issuing_unit" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Tag-Issuing Unit</field>
            <field name="privilege_id" ref="privilege_tag_issuing"/>
            <field name="comment">Confirms a printed label is physically affixed to the asset it names (INT-04/05). Grants the `tagging:write` API scope and nothing else. Independent of Asset Manager by design.</field>
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
